---
/**
 * Names the visitor's machine on the <html> element, before anything is drawn.
 *
 * WHY IT IS IN THE HEAD, INLINE, AND NOT A NORMAL COMPONENT SCRIPT.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The site is built ahead of time and served as files, so the markup that
 * leaves the server cannot know who asked for it. Every "Download for <your
 * machine>" on the page is therefore decided in the browser — and a decision
 * taken in an ordinary module script is taken AFTER the first paint, which
 * shows a Mac visitor "Download for Windows" for a frame and then swaps it.
 * That flicker is the whole reason the label was hardcoded before.
 *
 * An inline script in the head runs while the document is still being parsed,
 * before the body exists and before a single pixel is committed. It writes one
 * attribute; the labels are then chosen by CSS (`DownloadCta.astro`), so the
 * correct one is the only one the visitor ever sees.
 *
 * IT MUST STAY THE SIZE IT IS. This is the one script that blocks parsing, so
 * it does the minimum: two short tables of regular expressions and two
 * attributes. Anything that can wait belongs in a module script that does not
 * hold the page up.
 *
 * TWO ATTRIBUTES, BECAUSE THEY ANSWER TWO QUESTIONS. `data-os` picks the tab of
 * the install box — an iPhone lands on macOS there, which is the closest true
 * answer. `data-download` picks the installer the buttons at the top hand
 * over — and an iPhone gets none, because no desktop installer runs on it.
 * The rules for both live in `src/lib/install.ts`.
 *
 * WITHOUT JAVASCRIPT the attribute is simply never set, and the CSS falls back
 * to `DEFAULT_OS` — a real label pointing at a real command, not a blank
 * button. See docs/hero.md § "CTAs".
 *
 * `define:vars` is what carries the signature table across: an inline script
 * has no imports, so the table from `src/lib/install.ts` is serialised into the
 * markup instead of being retyped here. There is one copy of the rules.
 */
import { OS_SIGNATURES, DEFAULT_OS, DOWNLOAD_SIGNATURES, DEFAULT_DOWNLOAD } from "@/lib/install";
---

<script
  is:inline
  define:vars={{
    signatures: OS_SIGNATURES,
    fallback: DEFAULT_OS,
    downloads: DOWNLOAD_SIGNATURES,
    downloadFallback: DEFAULT_DOWNLOAD,
  }}
>
  (function () {
    var agent = navigator.userAgent || "";
    function pick(table, otherwise) {
      for (var i = 0; i < table.length; i++) {
        if (new RegExp(table[i][1], "i").test(agent)) return table[i][0];
      }
      return otherwise;
    }
    var download = pick(downloads, downloadFallback);
    // iPadOS reports itself as a Mac; its touch screen gives it away.
    if (download === "macos-arm64" && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 1) download = "other";
    var root = document.documentElement;
    root.setAttribute("data-os", pick(signatures, fallback));
    root.setAttribute("data-download", download);
  })();
</script>
